<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Karasids</h1><p class="st-meta">1297 CE – 1360 CE (approx.) · capital Balıkesir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A beylik on the Sea of Marmara and Aegean whose annexation gave the Ottomans the soldiers and ships to cross into Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around the end of the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karesi-bey">Karesi Bey</a> took the former Byzantine region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikesir">Balikesir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, as Byzantine defences in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> collapsed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Karasids raided across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Dardanelles</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, sometimes as allies of Byzantine factions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> met the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakhshi-bey">Yakhshi Bey</a>, in the 1330s. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karesi-bey">Karesi Bey's</a> death his sons, Demirhan and Yakhshi, divided the lands and quarrelled, opening the way to Ottoman intervention. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>'s warriors and commanders, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haci-ilbey">Haci Ilbey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evrenos-bey">Evrenos Bey</a>, later served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, a process usually dated to around 1345, though resistance continued for some years. Its manpower and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Dardanelles</a> coast enabled the Ottoman crossing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in 1354.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karasids.jpg" alt="Karasids" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Gabagool · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatolia1300.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1297 CE – 1360 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Balıkesir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karesi Bey, Demirhan Bey, Yakhshi Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karasid Beylik, Karesioğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1328&amp;focus=karasids" aria-label="Karasids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5368 758 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karasids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5688 1014L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5688 1014h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5688 1014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Balıkesir. <a href="/atlas?year=1328&amp;focus=karasids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

